  <!-- 3. 24-48H DELTA -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On June 14, Pakistan Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif, acting as principal mediator, announced that the United States and Iran had agreed on a memorandum of understanding to end the 111-day war, reopen the Strait of Hormuz, and open a 60-day window for nuclear negotiations. The framework — described by NPR and CNBC as less than two pages of text — covers a ceasefire, naval blockade lift, and deferred negotiations on enrichment limits and uranium stockpiles. The formal signing is scheduled for June 19 in Geneva, with US Vice President JD Vance and Iran Speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf as named signatories. Iran's IRNA carried President Pezeshkian's direct confirmation of the scheduled ceremony. The nuclear dossier — enrichment ceilings, HEU stockpile disposition, inspection regime — is explicitly deferred to the follow-on 60-day process. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>The June 14–16 diplomatic framework immediately ran into the Lebanon front. Israeli airstrikes near Nabatieh on June 16 killed four people; Iran's Khatam al-Anbiya Central Headquarters issued a public retaliation warning the same day. On June 17, Iran launched ten salvos of ballistic missiles at Israeli territory — the first direct Iranian strike against Israel since April — with confirmed impacts in Haifa (four killed) and Ramat Gan (two killed). Israel responded within hours with airstrikes on military installations in Tehran, Tabriz, and Isfahan; Iranian media confirmed explosions at those sites and at the Karun petrochemical complex in Mahshahr. Both sides halted further exchanges following reported US pressure on Netanyahu. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Lebanon now constitutes the main structural fault line in the MoU. Iran's MFA spokesman Esmail Baghaei stated that "the end of the war in Lebanon is an inseparable part of the agreement to end the war," while US Ambassador Mike Huckabee explicitly stated that Hezbollah is not covered by the US-Iran deal and that Israel retains the right of self-defense in Lebanon. This gap — Iran demanding a Lebanon ceasefire as a condition, the US rejecting that linkage — is the live threat to the June 19 signing. Ghalibaf's delegation on June 14 stated there was "no point in continuing talks" following a Beirut strike before apparently walking back that position; Witkoff and Araghchi were working on a June 17 face-to-face session to stabilize the channel. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>On Hormuz, the partial physical opening is proceeding ahead of the formal ceremony. CENTCOM confirmed the deployment of USS Frank E. Peterson (DDG 121) and USS Michael Murphy (DDG 112) — the first US warships to transit Hormuz since the war began — alongside Avenger-class minesweepers and autonomous underwater vehicles conducting sonar sweeps. Trump stated the US Navy had escorted 200 commercial ships through the southern Oman corridor over the prior month. JMIC downgraded the threat rating from "critical" to "severe" on June 7. CNBC (June 15) cited tanker CEO projections of approximately 12 vessels per day within 30 days of a signed deal — a figure that falls below both the <span class="cite">N2</span> threshold (19/day) and the <span class="cite">F4</span> threshold (50/day) and arrives after both deadlines. The mine clearance timeline — 40 days at minimum, six months for a comprehensive sweep — is the binding physical constraint on transit recovery regardless of diplomatic pace. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>Israel's domestic political calendar remained static in the June 10–18 window. No second or third reading of the Knesset dissolution bill has been scheduled as of June 18. The coalition continues operating at approximately 60 seats following UTJ's departure over the Haredi conscription dispute; Netanyahu has not lost a formal confidence vote. The election date band (September 8–October 20) remains subject to internal negotiation, with Netanyahu seeking an October slot. The June 16–17 bilateral exchange produced no reported adjustment to the dissolution timeline. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 4. SCENARIO MAP -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:30%;">Escalation 30%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:52%;">Protracted 52%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:18%;">De-esc 18%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (30%):</strong> The June 19 MoU signing fails or is repudiated within days — driven by an Israeli strike on Hezbollah triggering IRGC retaliation under Iran's Lebanon linkage demand — collapsing N1, N3, and N4 simultaneously and reactivating the broader conflict above current tempo.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (52%):</strong> The MoU is signed June 19 but the Lebanon fault line produces recurring tactical exchanges, nuclear talks (N4) commence but stall on frozen-funds and enrichment disputes, and Hormuz reopens partially on naval-escort corridors while commercial traffic (N2) remains below threshold through the 40-day MCM floor.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (18%):</strong> N1 signs, Lebanon operations pause under sustained US pressure giving N3 its 72-hour window, N4 commences with at least one working-level technical session before July 19, and MCM progress on Hormuz produces commercial traffic trends that make later-horizon recovery forecasts reachable.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA carried President Pezeshkian's direct statement that "the memorandum of understanding to end the war between Iran and America is scheduled to be signed on Friday," framing the MoU as an Iranian diplomatic achievement with named terms: a 60-day ceasefire, Hormuz reopening, and deferred nuclear negotiations. Foreign Minister Araghchi's public framing via Tasnim was noticeably cautionary — "We have seen agreements go unimplemented, and we have seen agreements torn up" — hedging the domestic presentation against the possibility of US non-compliance. MFA spokesman Baghaei's insistence that Lebanon "is an inseparable part of the agreement" functions simultaneously as a diplomatic demand and a domestic legitimacy anchor, allowing the government to claim the MoU extends Iranian protection to Lebanese allies while conditioning acceptance on an outcome the US has rejected. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>Tasnim and Fars gave prominent coverage to the June 17 missile salvo against Israel as a legitimate and proportionate act of self-defense — "retaliation for Israeli crimes in Lebanon." IRGC Khatam al-Anbiya Headquarters' June 16 public warning statement was broadcast across all state channels before the launch, constructing a legal and political narrative of proportionality ahead of the strike. Damage assessments from Israeli media — confirmed impacts in Haifa and Ramat Gan residential areas — were not featured; instead state coverage concentrated on IRGC targeting of "Ramat David Airbase," mirroring the framing pattern used in earlier rounds of the conflict to maintain a military-target narrative domestically. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Press TV treated CENTCOM's mine clearance deployment as evidence that the United States formally acknowledged Iran's successful closure of Hormuz for 111 days, casting demining as a US concession validating Iranian leverage. Kayhan, the hardline daily, ran commentary questioning whether the 60-day MoU framework represents a genuine US commitment or a tactical pause designed to enable reconstitution — citing Araghchi's own warning about past agreements being abandoned as internal corroboration. The frozen-funds precondition for nuclear talks was presented across Tasnim and IRNA as a non-negotiable sovereignty matter: Iran frames the release of frozen assets as overdue restitution for prior US sanctions violations, not as a negotiating concession, a framing that constrains the Iranian delegation's room to accept a phased or conditional release. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

