        <div class="drivers">Economic attrition from 111-day Hormuz closure and war costs has eroded middle-class purchasing power; the MoU, if it holds, partially alleviates pressure but does not resolve the frozen-assets and sanctions architecture. The Supreme Leader's public silence on the MoU text is the leading internal risk indicator — Kayhan's skepticism tracks a hardliner coalition that could exploit any US non-compliance to delegitimize the Pezeshkian government's concessions. (Inherited from 2026-06-10; next review 2026-06-21.)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="regime-row">
        <span class="name">🇮🇱 Israel (Netanyahu Steps Down)</span>
        <span class="p">30–42%</span>
        <div class="drivers">Coalition erosion to approximately 60 seats following UTJ departure, dissolution bill advancing unanimously through first reading (106-0), and polling at 49 projected seats constitute the structural pressure. F6 (Knesset sets election date by July 15, p=45%) and F7 (coalition collapse by October 27, p=35%) together bracket a high-probability scenario in which Netanyahu faces elections before the MoU's 60-day nuclear window closes. The 30–42% step-down range reflects that elections are likely but Netanyahu's departure depends on electoral outcome and coalition arithmetic post-vote.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

